<title identifier="/us/usc/t99"><num value="99">Title 99</num><heading>Invented</heading>
<chapter identifier="/us/usc/t99/ch1"><num value="1">CHAPTER 1—</num><heading>WIDGETS</heading>
<section identifier="/us/usc/t99/s1"><num value="1">§ 1.</num><heading>Conditions</heading>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s1/a"><num value="a">(a)</num><content>In the case of a gadget, for purposes of this section, the term “sprocket” means a toothed wheel.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s1/b"><num value="b">(b)</num><content>For purposes of this section, in the case of a gadget, the term “cog” means a tooth.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s1/c"><num value="c">(c)</num><content>As used in this section, the term “gear”, in the case of a gadget, means a wheel.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s1/d"><num value="d">(d)</num><content>The term “spindle” means, in the case of a gadget, a rod.</content></subsection>
</section>
<section identifier="/us/usc/t99/s2"><num value="2">§ 2.</num><heading>Conditions in chapeaus</heading><chapeau>In this section:</chapeau>
<paragraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s2/1"><num value="1">(1)</num><chapeau>In the case of a gadget—</chapeau>
<subparagraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s2/1/A"><num value="A">(A)</num><content>The term “lever” means a bar.</content></subparagraph>
</paragraph>
<paragraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s2/2"><num value="2">(2)</num><chapeau>In the case of a widget, in this paragraph:</chapeau>
<subparagraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s2/2/A"><num value="A">(A)</num><content>The term “pulley” means a wheel with a rope.</content></subparagraph>
</paragraph>
</section>
<section identifier="/us/usc/t99/s3"><num value="3">§ 3.</num><heading>Borrowed meanings</heading>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s3/a"><num value="a">(a)</num><content>The term “doodad” has the meaning given that term by the Doodad Act of 1999.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s3/b"><num value="b">(b)</num><content>The term “ratchet” has the meaning given such term in regulations prescribed under <ref href="/us/usc/t99/s1">section 1 of this title</ref>.</content></subsection>
</section>
<section identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4"><num value="4">§ 4.</num><heading>Sentences, blocks and parts</heading>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4/a"><num value="a">(a)</num><content>A gizmo is small. For purposes of this section, the term “gizmo” means a small gadget.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4/b"><num value="b">(b)</num><content><p>For purposes of this subsection:</p><p>“thingamajig” means a part;</p><p>“whatsit” means a piece.</p></content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4/c"><num value="c">(c)</num><chapeau>A gizmo is—</chapeau>
<paragraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4/c/1"><num value="1">(1)</num><content>small; or</content></paragraph>
<paragraph identifier="/us/usc/t99/s4/c/2"><num value="2">(2)</num><content>light.</content></paragraph>
<continuation>For purposes of this subsection, the term “gizmo” includes a toy.</continuation>
</subsection>
</section>
<section identifier="/us/usc/t99/s5"><num value="5">§ 5.</num><heading>The text the rules read</heading>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s5/a"><num value="a">(a)</num><content>As used in this section, the terms “gizmo’ ” and “ ‘new’ gizmo” mean small gadgets.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s5/b"><num value="b">(b)</num><content>In this section,<sup>1</sup> the term “doohickey” means a small part.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s5/c"><num value="c">(c)</num><content>For purposes of section 2 <sup>1</sup> of this title, the term “whatnot” means a tool.</content></subsection>
<subsection identifier="/us/usc/t99/s5/d"><num value="d">(d)</num><content>For purposes of section 2 1 of this title, the term “gimcrack” means a toy.</content></subsection>
</section>
</chapter>
